From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 07/15] dndcp: Copy and paste between host and guest in a
 Wayland session

Copy and paste between host and guest does not work in a Wayland
session. The plugin drives the X11 selection directly, and from an
Xwayland client that does not reach native Wayland applications on every
compositor. A compositor also gives the Wayland selection only to a
client with keyboard focus, and vmusr has no window.

Add a Wayland backend, CopyPasteDnDWayland, with the copy and paste UI
in CopyPasteUIWayland. CopyPasteDnDWrapper chooses it at run time when
WAYLAND_DISPLAY is set and the session is not an X11 one, or when there
is no X display. A compositor started from a text console, such as sway,
leaves XDG_SESSION_TYPE at "tty", and an X11 session that happens to
export WAYLAND_DISPLAY says "x11". The backend is built for both the
GTK3 and the GTK4 configuration, when configure has found the Wayland
libraries.

The UI takes the selection through waylandClipboard/ where the
compositor implements ext-data-control-v1, as KWin and wlroots do. It
offers every format the host sent and answers each request with the
bytes for the requested type. One selection then carries a PNG image,
RTF and the plain text rendering at once, as the X11 backend does with
separate selection targets. Without data control, copy and paste is off.
The next patch covers GNOME.

The host sends its clipboard again each time the guest gets the focus,
changed or not. Installing it again would make a new selection, which a
clipboard manager such as Klipper treats as a new copy. The UI leaves
the selection alone while it still holds the same host clip.

The backend also answers the host's requests for the guest clipboard. It
reads an image first, then RTF with its plain text rendering, then plain
text, as copyPasteUIX11.cpp does. Every request gets exactly one reply,
with an empty or not-changed clip when there is nothing new.

The host asks for the guest clipboard every few seconds while the
pointer crosses the VM's edge. The backend reads the selection only
after it has changed, going by the count of selection events that
waylandClipboard/ keeps. A selection that this process owns is not read
back. A changed selection can still hold what was sent last, when the
same text is copied again or a clipboard manager takes over the
selection, so the content is compared too.

Reads stop at a deadline instead of blocking. The main loop also carries
the RPC channel, and an owner that stops writing must not stall it. All
the reads for one host request share a 2 s budget. A read that runs out
of time is tried once more on the next request, and after that only when
the selection changes again.

The cache of what was last sent is updated only once the item is on the
outgoing clip, and a new host clip clears it. Updating it earlier would
make a failed send look unchanged on the next attempt, and that content
could then never be copied again. The backend reads the guest selection
once at start-up, so that an old guest clip does not look new and
overwrite a newer host clipboard as soon as the VM gets focus.

Drag and drop stays with DnDUIX11, for which the backend pins GDK to its
X11 backend, so the detection window is an Xwayland client. gtk_init()
exits the process when it cannot open a display, and a Wayland session
may have no Xwayland. The backend calls gtk_init_check() first and
leaves drag and drop off without GTK. vmusr as shipped still needs the
display, because the desktopEvents plugin, which loads first, calls
gtk_init() itself. The backend does not advertise drag and drop, which a
later patch makes work under Xwayland. It advertises copy and paste of
plain text, RTF and PNG images.
